THE READING.
Read 2026-11-16, the post entire — the abstract, the
diagnosis, the evolution, the unseen-suite tables, the
kernel engineering, the multi-GPU benchmarks with their
stated caveats, and the use and citation sections, at
the author's own site. Staged at
~/workspace/s015-temp/blog-text.md.

WHAT IT IS.
A research blog post with its artifact shipped: an
evolved revision of Meta's e-means clustering
(arXiv:2611.09501, days old when the post landed), found
by running EvoForest (arXiv:2604.19761) over the
algorithm's own modular graph for nine rounds, then
engineered into fused GPU kernels and a
scikit-learn-shaped estimator, both in the open at
github.com/suayptalha/s-means. The post is the paper of
the work: its tables are the results of record, and its
citation block names three authors — Kocabay, Yüksel and
Yalçın.

WHO.
Şuayp Talha Kocabay — the post stands at his site, the
code in his repository, the name at its head his. The
check on the author stands at 40.1.4.

THE SHAPE OF THE POST.
A diagnosis of four bottlenecks in the upstream design;
the evolution that answered them, told as a trajectory
of versions with their scores; the resulting algorithm
stated whole; a generalization test on datasets the
evolution never saw, with the artifact taken apart piece
by piece; the GPU engineering; benchmarks on three
cards, with the boundaries stated in the open — the
workloads where the artifact is slower, and the upstream
failures on the oldest card; and the use of the thing,
with its tests.
# Study S015 — the mechanism: a modular graph, evolved
under a guarded judge

THE GROUND — E-MEANS AS BUILT.
Meta's design (1.2.317), in the four parts as the post
names them: random rows for the first centres; revival
of dead clusters every five steps when a cluster's use
falls below a tenth of the mean use, off by default at
dimension 2 and below; splits of busy donors by a jitter
of multiplication, c * (1 ± 1e-4 * r); and the final
pass averaged plain, sums over counts. Its knobs — batch
and step rate — come off the scaling-law recipe
(1.2.318).

THE SEARCH.
The algorithm is rewritten as a graph of seven named
parts — assign_cfg, recipe_fn, init_fn, schedule_fn,
update_fn, revive_fn, finalize_fn — and EvoForest
(1.2.326) runs on CPU: four islands, nine rounds,
thirty-six island steps, against the canonical e-means
as the starting graph. The judge: eight training
datasets chosen to span failure modes — stretched
Gaussians, isotropic mixtures, a Zipf imbalance, a
two-dimensional set built at several scales, a
hypersphere, a correlated set shifted by +50, and
California housing raw and standardized. Every candidate
gets exactly the epoch budget of the benchmark. The
score is 65% held-out error and 35% train error, guarded
by a worst-case regression penalty, a runtime penalty
past 1.05x of the baseline, and a scratch-memory penalty
past 1.02x.

THE TRAJECTORY, AS THE POST TELLS IT.
v0, the baseline, scores 0.064. Step 3 (v1): revival by
distortion — the error track kept, the split radius made
shift-invariant, revival extended to dimension 1 —
10.76. Step 6 (v3): the D^1.5 seeding — 16.09. Step 10
(v4): the feature scale inside the update, making the
splits anisotropic — 18.73. Step 15 (v5): cache tiling
and the four-round seeding — 20.47. Step 23 (v6): the
progressive final-pass weights — 20.69. And v8: the
empirical-Bayes finalize — 20.80 — with round 8
rejecting all four of its candidates: the convergence of
the search, stated as a clean rejection round.

THE RESULTING ALGORITHM.
Stated whole at the concepts: the seeding (1.2.321), the
revival (1.2.322), the ending (1.2.323), the recipe kept
with a power-cosine decay laid over the rate — the
cosine term raised to the power 0.92, floored at 0.06 of
the opening rate — and the whole carrying O(Kd) of extra
state and no extra pass over the data.
# Study S015 — the worked core: the numbers, and the one
run of our own

THE UNSEEN SUITE (the table in section 3 of the post;
1.2.327).
Twelve datasets the evolution never trained on, five
seeds each. The mean held-out gain over e-means is
+19.67% (train +21.30%), with no dataset going backward
on the held-out measure — the smallest row is
covtype_std at exactly +0.00%. The gains concentrate
where the diagnosis said they would: the isotropic
mixture at +62.69%, the large 128-dimension run at
+57.20%, the fractional-budget run at +54.21%, the
high-dimension Zipf at +37.62% — and thin out on the
near-solved rows (digits at +0.03% and +0.50%, the
uniform cube at +0.27%). The time price: a median of
0.977x of e-means' time, and a median 2.24x faster than
MiniBatchKMeans at matched budgets — faster on eight of
the twelve, slower on the small and low-dimensional
rows, as the post states. Memory: no extra resident
memory measured, 0.0 MB of delta on the two workloads
priced by process telemetry.

THE ABLATION (Figure 5 of the post, right panel).
The revival removed: the mean gain falls from +19.86% to
+7.09% — 12.77 points, the load-bearing part priced. The
seeding removed: -2.64 points. Revival restricted to
dimension 16 and up: -1.19 points. The tiling removed:
the gain unchanged, the speed gone. One tension stands
in the source and is carried as it stands: the ablation
prices the whole at +19.86% where the table of the suite
itself aggregates +19.67%; the post does not reconcile
the two figures.

THE GPU NUMBERS (section 5 of the post).
Nine workloads, three seeds, three cards: mean held-out
gains of +54.03% (T4, Triton), +54.35% (L4), +55.21%
(A100), the largest single row +78.71% on the 100k-point
Gaussian mixture. The epoch pair the title rests on: at
200k x 128D, k=1024, s-means at one epoch reaches 32.21
MSE in 33 ms on A100, where e-means at eight epochs
stands at 65.68 in 36 ms — one pass beating eight, at
less time. Peak workspace: a median 0.875x of e-means'
Triton on the two newer cards; 71.2 MB against 84.6 at
the million-point row, and against 309.8 for the native
path. On the T4 the upstream paths fail in the open — a
RuntimeError on FP32 inputs, a PTX error on the .bf16
instruction, a shared-memory refusal at 98,304 against
65,536 — while s-means runs all nine workloads on both
its backends. The stated boundaries: on the small
low-dimension workload the artifact is the slower by
near six times (567.4 ms against the graph-captured 95.3
of the upstream on A100), its eager loop against
captured graphs — accuracy and memory still its own
(+23.4%, 0.875x) — and on the T4 its Triton path buys
memory (0.360x median) at 4.586x median latency.

THE ESTIMATOR.
The package passes the complete check_estimator suite of
scikit-learn as its author reports it (pytest: 10
passed), and carries no dependency on the upstream
e_means code.

OUR OWN RUN — THE SPOT-CHECK.
Run by Emise on aletheia-core, 2026-11-16, from the
repository as it stands (torch 2.15.0+cpu, scikit-learn
1.9.1): the published estimator installed clean and fit,
against MiniBatchKMeans at the matched-budget rule of
the post itself, on the digits configuration of the
unseen suite of the post (64 dimensions, k=64, T=5,
standardized, an 80/20 split, three seeds). Held-out
error: s-means 33.38, 25.25, 25.74 against
MiniBatchKMeans' 37.09, 28.87, 24.19 — a mean gain of
+6.40%, with the third seed against it (-6.40%). What
this decides, and no more: the artifact exists,
installs, runs as documented, and on one configuration
of the suite itself stands in the same range as — on
mean, ahead of — a matched classical baseline. The
twelve-dataset headline, and every GPU number, rest on
the author's runs still: they are filed as his report,
and the outside run that would decide them stands as
Q123.
