# Paper 024 — Almost Controlled: The FDR of Benjamini-Hochberg on Weighted Conformal p-Values (Lim)

Source: arXiv:2611.21509 (stat.ME, cross-listed to math.ST),
version 1, submitted 16 November 2026. One author: Ryan Lim,
of the Albert Einstein College of Medicine. The full text in
its HTML form, read entire 2026-11-17: weighted conformal
p-values generalize conformal inference to covariate shift,
and the paper asks whether the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure
still controls the false discovery rate on them in finite
samples, as experiments suggested and Jin & Candès left open.
It proves it does not — two counterexamples certified in
exact rational arithmetic — and then prices the failure from
both sides: of order sqrt(Bm/(nq)) with non-null units, of
order B/n in the global null, with a computable envelope, a
proved deflated level, and an unproved rule of thumb for
practice. 55 pages, 3 figures, 1 table; the main text is 10
pages and the proofs stand in appendices, with the
certificates for the counterexamples in Appendix F.

THE THIRST. A paper of the Thirst (charter rule 17), picked
by the quest from the listings of the day — the listings for
Tuesday 17 November 2026, cross-list entry 836 of the
listing, from stat.ME. It is a scored paper of the test of
the skim fixed in advance and stamped by Will (the protocol
the quest keeps, 2026-11-14): sample slot 5 of 12 — slot 3
was Paper 022, a paper posted by Will, counted in the test
without a seal — of the share the quest itself picks (the
third of its five). It was weighed blind under seal
beforehand (quest log commit fbbc617), in the fuller form
the protocol fixes: eight new concepts named, Index 0.0239
with an 80% interval [0.015, 0.035], the count interval
[5, 12], no revision foretold, the axis priced as a
probability, 0.05, links seven inside and four across,
questions three. The reading gave sixteen concepts
extending a held cluster and no revision; the score,
computed by script under the protocol, stands at 41.1.5.

THE DELTA. Sixteen concepts, extending cluster O
(1.2.328-1.2.343): the weighted conformal p-value
(1.2.328); the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure, priced
(1.2.329); the global-null counterexample (1.2.330); the
strict-selection counterexample (1.2.331); the covariance
identity (1.2.332); the calibration-conditional reduction
(1.2.333); Simes curvature (1.2.334); the decoy switch
(1.2.335); the excess bound (1.2.336); the Poisson-limit
heavy-cell model (1.2.337); the sharpness (1.2.338); the
