# Paper 025 — author check (charter rule 15): no phext
tie and no use without naming found, by checks Emise
ran at intake

THE QUESTION. Two things are asked of the author of a
work taken in: whether he stands in any recorded tie
to phext or to Will's work, and whether the work uses
Will's earlier published work without naming it. The
check on Sa'ar Zehavi was run in three legs on
2026-11-17. It is the thirty-third check that stands
run.

LEG ONE — the work itself. The full text of
arXiv:2611.18218 was searched for the signature terms:
phext, scroll, Exocortex, Mirrorborn, coordinate
addressing, Bickford, CYOA, SBOR. No occurrence of any
of them. The reference list (35 entries) was read in
full: it is the Chabauty-Kim and Iwasawa literature —
Kim, Coates-Kim, Corwin-Dan-Cohen, Balakrishnan and
co-authors, Lim, Ardakov-Wadsley, Milne, Faltings,
Filip — and holds no work of Will's.

LEG TWO — the web. Eleven searches were run on the
author's name against the adjacency terms (phext,
Exocortex, Mirrorborn, wbic16, Will Bickford, SBOR,
CYOA, phext.io) and on the paper's number. No result
connects the author to phext, to Will Bickford, to the
Exocortex, to Mirrorborn, to CYOA, or to SBOR. The
searches do settle his identity, and it is recorded
because the check asked: the author of this paper is
the Chabauty-Kim number theorist of the same name —
doctorate at Tel Aviv University (2018-2022) under
Zeev Rudnick; postdoctoral fellow at Ben-Gurion
University, in Ishai Dan-Cohen's group (Israel Science
Foundation grant 621/21, Dan-Cohen principal
investigator — Dan-Cohen is a cited predecessor and
the author's grant principal investigator, not a
co-author); then the Weizmann Institute; the
affiliation on this paper is the Max Planck Institute
for Mathematics. His earlier Chabauty-Kim works stand
in the paper's own references: with Corwin, the
unipotent Chabauty-Kim-Kantor method (2024), and with
Jarossay, Lilienfeldt, Saettone and Weiss, the
polylogarithmic motivic Chabauty-Kim paper (2024). One
electronic address, saarzehavi@gmail.com, runs through
the record from 2017 to this paper.

LEG THREE — the archive. Will's Twitter archive was
searched entire (all eleven files): Zehavi occurs zero
times in every file, in every variant tried. Saar as a
standing word occurs zero times in the tweets; the
character string occurs only inside media addresses, in
one doubled handle (x.com/saarsaar) with nothing
connecting it to the author, and as a single bare word
in one group message of 2025 with no surname and no
identifying context. No occurrence of the author is
found.

THE VERDICT. No evidence found — of any tie to phext
or to Will, and of any use of Will's work, named or
unnamed. The finding is a null finding and is recorded
as one: it accuses the author of nothing, and it
certifies nothing beyond the reach of the three legs
named above.
# Paper 025 — synthesis: the error term, priced in
another country [synthesis]

[synthesis] The book's papers keep arriving at the same
shape: a main term somebody wants, and an error term
nobody has priced. Cluster O's judges were priced by
their corrections; Paper 024 priced a guarantee by the
covariance its own inputs created. This paper is the
shape at its largest scale. Kim's inequality compares
two dimensions and the whole difficulty lives in one
summand — dim H^2, the obstruction term, the part of
the Selmer side that is not accounted for by points or
by local conditions. Zehavi's move is to refuse to
bound it where it lives. The H^2 is dual to maps out of
one Iwasawa module; the maps are kernels of operators;
the operators live, after two changes of ring, in an
enveloping algebra whose graded growth is the geometry
of a nilpotent cone. The error term is priced in a
country three translations away from the curve, and
the price is exact enough — a power saving — to settle
a fifteen-year-old conjecture.

[synthesis] The generality is the same lesson read from
the other side. Earlier proofs of cases of the
conjecture worked by knowing the Jacobian: complex
multiplication, large Galois image, an endomorphism
ring one could compute with. This proof knows two
facts about the Galois image — it is open in its own
closure, and the closure's Lie algebra holds the
scalars — and after its second section it never looks
at the curve again. What the book files as 1.2.359 is
worth carrying as a method, beyond this country: a
theorem about every object of a kind is often cheaper
than a theorem about one object whose properties must
first be established. Price the ambient structure, and
the cases take care of themselves.

[synthesis] For the house, one caution stands beside
the admiration. The tracker files this paper's claims
attributed, and the delta says plainly that nothing
was re-proved: the book now holds a settled conjecture
of arithmetic geometry entirely on the say-so of a
single paper, itself resting on a shelf of cited work,
one part of its development — by the author's own
declaration, recorded at 42.1.1 — carried out in
back-and-forth with a language model and then rewritten
by the author alone. That is the ordinary condition of
a literature-facing tracker, not a scandal; but the
registry's honesty is that the standing says attributed
and means it. If the proof is ever found wanting, the
sixteen concepts of cluster AI move together, and the
receipt that moves them will be written at 1.4.6 like
any other.
# Paper 025 — Open questions Q130-Q133

Q130 — effectivity. Theorem 1.1's finite bad-weight set
E is the union of the root sets of the annihilators'
polynomials, and neither E, nor any implied constant,
nor an n_0 from which Corollary 1.5's inequality holds
is computed anywhere in the paper. An effective form —
E listed, the constant of Theorem 1.4 explicit, an
n_0(p, S, C) named — would turn the finiteness of the
Chabauty-Kim loci into a bound a computation could
spend. (Opened by Paper 025, the statements of
Theorems 1.1 and 1.4 and of Corollary 1.5.)

Q131 — the prime 2. The proof uses that p is odd at
four named points: the unique square roots of the
uniform splitting (Theorem 2.4), the p-cohomological
dimension step of Proposition 2.14, the archimedean
step of Proposition 2.17 (the Brauer group of R is
Z/2), and the very-goodness of odd primes for type
C_g behind Theorem 3.11. Whether the dimension
inequality holds at p = 2, and by what route, stands
open. (Opened by Paper 025, sections 2 and 3.)

Q132 — the quasi-projective case. Kim's Conjecture 1.3
as quoted in the paper is stated for smooth
quasi-projective curves; this paper settles the smooth
projective geometrically connected case. The punctured
case stands settled only in the special families the
paper cites (the thrice-punctured line, solvable covers
of P^1, the CM families) and open in general.
(Opened by Paper 025, its section 1.1.)

Q133 — the exponent in the restatement of Theorem 1.4.
At its restatement inside section 4.1, the paper prints
the power saving with the exponent -1/(2(D+1)); the
statement in section 1, the summary in section 1.2.2,
and both computations of the balance (sections 1.2.2
and 4.1) give -1/(D+1). The filing takes the proved
exponent (1.2.346). Whether the restatement is a
misprint, or a weaker bound the author intended at
that point, stands open until the author or a later
version settles it. (Opened by Paper 025, found by the
reading at this taking-in.)
